<!--
Placeholders:
  {{TARGET_FRAMEWORK}}  — the TFM the projects already use (e.g. net10.0). ONLY when the user
                          explicitly opted into centralising it; otherwise delete that line.

Repo root. MSBuild applies this to every project below it — no .csproj edits needed.

CHECK FOR A NESTED COPY FIRST.
MSBuild walks up from each project and stops at the first Directory.Build.props it finds. One at
src/Directory.Build.props makes a root-level file dead for everything under src/ — in a repo laid
out as src/<Layer>/<Project>, that is every project, and nothing reports an error. If a nested file
exists, either merge into it instead of writing here, or add this line to it so the root file is
chained:

  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove($(MSBuildThisFileName)$(MSBuildThisFileExtension), $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..))" />

Either way, confirm the properties are actually in force:

  dotnet msbuild <any-project> -getProperty:TreatWarningsAsErrors

TARGET FRAMEWORK — DELETE THE LINE UNLESS THE USER OPTED IN.
This file is imported before the body of each .csproj, so a project that declares its own
TargetFramework overrides whatever is set here. Since virtually every project created from a
template declares one, the property below is inert: it reads as "the TFM is centralised" while each
project still decides for itself.

Centralising it for real means deleting TargetFramework from every .csproj — the same class of
migration as central package management, and offered the same way. Off by default. Always delete
the line when any project multi-targets (TargetFrameworks) or when projects disagree.

NEVER PUT TWO CONSECUTIVE HYPHENS IN A COMMENT.
XML forbids the sequence inside a comment, so MSBuild cannot load the file. It is easy to hit here,
because the skill asks you to justify every exception and command-line flags are the natural way to
name one. Write the flag as prose instead: "locked mode", not the flag spelling; "the no-restore
build", not the flag spelling.

The failure gives you nothing to work with. `dotnet restore` reports

  MSB4181: The "RestoreTask" task returned false but did not log an error

with no file and no line. If a build starts failing right after this file is edited, validate it
before investigating anything else:

  dotnet msbuild <any-project> -getProperty:TargetFramework

That surfaces the real XML error, naming the file and the line.

ADOPTION NOTE — repositories with history.
Turning TreatWarningsAsErrors on cold surfaces years of accumulated warnings. Ship it with an
explicit NoWarn list and drain the list over time; the file then IS the debt register. Every entry
needs a reason and an owner:

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1062;IDE0290</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

Delete this header before writing the file.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Only when the team opted into centralising the target framework. -->
    <TargetFramework>{{TARGET_FRAMEWORK}}</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A warning stops being an opinion. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <!-- .editorconfig rules run inside the build, not only in whoever's IDE has them on. -->
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <!-- Required for IDE0005 (unnecessary using directives) to be reported on a command-line
         build. Without it Roslyn skips the rule and warns that this property is missing, so the
         style gate quietly loses one of its most useful rules. dotnet/roslyn issue 41640. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      A vulnerable dependency breaks the build.

      This is the one setting here that can turn a green commit red with nobody touching the
      repository: a newly published advisory against a dependency you already have fails the next
      build. That is intended — a known vulnerability should stop the line — but it is a decision
      the team accepts, not a default. To soften it, raise NuGetAuditLevel to moderate or set
      NuGetAuditMode to direct, and record the reason right here.
    -->
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Freeze the resolved dependency tree, transitives included. The generated
         packages.lock.json files belong in version control: CI restores in locked mode and fails
         when they drift from the projects. Drop this line if the team opted out of lock files. -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Same inputs, same binary. -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(TF_BUILD)' == 'true' or '$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
